\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa80{\cf1\hich\ai0\dbch\af11\loch\i0
Keywords: acidophile; growth pH limit; comparative genomics; periplasm; surface charge; phylogenetic generalised least squares}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240

\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240

\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\pagebb\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa80\keepn{\cf1\hich\afs24\ab\dbch\af11\loch\fs24\b
Abstract}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033{\cf1\b
Background. }{\cf1
The genomic correlates of reported minimum growth pH may differ from those of growth optimum. We tested whether charge composition on the outward-facing surfaces of conserved membrane proteins is associated with reported minimum growth pH after adjustment for growth optimum.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033{\cf1\b
Methods. }{\cf1
We analysed 2,768 bacterial genomes using a 32-reference membrane-protein panel, structural confidence and solvent-accessibility filters, and phylogenetic generalised least squares with genome-wide composition and source controls. Matched cytoplasmic and transmembrane positions provided compartment controls. Models incorporating growth optimum used the same 1,655 genomes for each comparison.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033{\cf1\b
Results. }{\cf1
The extracytoplasmic net acidic composite was positively associated with reported minimum growth pH (+0.1848 pH units per standard deviation; P = 2 \u215\'d7 10\u8315\'3f\u185\'b9\u8304\'3f). The cytoplasmic and transmembrane controls were not statistically significant. A separate pooled residue-share analysis adjusted for growth optimum and exposed histidine retained 39% of its own baseline association (+0.1120 per standard deviation; P = 4 \u215\'d7 10\u8315\'3f\u179\'b3). Seven of 22 detectable reference families passed Benjamini\u8211\'96Hochberg correction at q < 0.10. The composite did not improve prediction in held-out phyla.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033{\cf1\b
Conclusions. }{\cf1
Acid-tolerant bacteria have lower net acidic composition on extracytoplasmic-facing positions of this reference panel. The result is an observational association; most reported minima are not experimentally demonstrated boundaries. A charge-burden interpretation is a hypothesis requiring experimental testing.}
\par \pard\plain \s1\sl480\slmult1\keep\sb240\sa80\keepn\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs28\ab\ltrch\hich\af5\afs28\ab\dbch\af10\loch\f5\fs28\b{\cf1\hich\af8\afs28\dbch\af11\loch\f8\fs28
Introduction}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240{\cf1
Bacteria differ greatly in the acidity at which growth is observed. Some }{\cf1\i
Acidithiobacillus}{\cf1
 isolates grow below pH 1; the archaeal genus }{\cf1\i
Picrophilus}{\cf1
 also includes organisms capable of growth at very low pH. A reported minimum growth pH is distinct from the growth optimum. A species with an optimum near pH 7 may have reported growth extending to pH 4 or only to pH 6. Here we study that reported minimum, without assuming it is a demonstrated biological boundary.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240{\cf1\hich\ai0\dbch\af11\loch\i0
The mechanisms of acid resistance that are understood in detail are inducible and physiological. Enteric bacteria surviving gastric transit deploy amino-acid decarboxylase systems that consume cytoplasmic protons, regulated by sensors that detect acidification directly: the EvgS/EvgA two-component system, in which a single histidine in the extracytoplasmic domain is required for acid induction [1], and the regulator AdiY, whose pH response runs through the protonation of two histidines [2]. }{\cf1\hich\ai\dbch\af11\loch\i
Helicobacter pylori}{\cf1\hich\ai0\dbch\af11\loch\i0
 additionally imports urea through the pH-gated channel UreI to feed intrabacterial urease [3]. Experimental evolution for acid resistance in }{\cf1\hich\ai\dbch\af11\loch\i
Escherichia coli}{\cf1\hich\ai0\dbch\af11\loch\i0
 converges on the same signalling machinery, with evgS mutated in every selected population [4]. These are responses: machinery switched on when the environment turns acidic, and they explain survival of acid shock better than they explain the pH at which steady growth becomes impossible.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240{\cf1\hich\ai0\dbch\af11\loch\i0
A limit might instead be set by what the cell's proteins can tolerate, and if so the relevant compartment is identifiable in advance. A Gram-negative bacterium holds its cytoplasm near neutrality even in strong acid, so cytoplasmic proteins never experience the external pH. The periplasm has no such protection: it equilibrates far closer to the medium, and proteins with surfaces facing it operate at whatever pH the organism is growing in. Any compositional signature of acid adaptation should therefore appear on outward-facing surfaces and not on cytoplasmic ones \u8212\'97 which makes the hypothesis testable against a control that is the same measurement on a different face of the same protein. Consistent with the compartment mattering, extracytoplasmic redox proteins of acidophiles have systematically more positive redox potentials than neutrophilic homologues [5].}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240{\cf1\hich\ai0\dbch\af11\loch\i0
Compositional signatures of other extremes are established; for pH they are not. Thermophily is visible in the IVYWREL fraction and halophily in a pronounced excess of surface acidic residues, while a review of archaeal extremophile protein adaptation reports that adaptations toward pH are considerably less discernible [6]. That is a documented absence rather than an assumption, and it is the gap addressed here.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240{\cf1\hich\ai0\dbch\af11\loch\i0
Two recent results make the question tractable. Cabello-Yeves et al. showed that predicted isoelectric point shifts with habitat between marine and freshwater prokaryotes, with the difference sharper in secreted than cytoplasmic than membrane proteins and concentrated on protein surfaces [7], establishing that compartment-resolved composition carries environmental signal. Independently, Barnum et al. showed that growth pH can be predicted from amino acid composition computed per predicted subcellular localization, reporting the extracellular-minus-intracellular glutamate difference as the strongest residue-level correlate [8]. Together these say that the compartment-resolved signal exists and that pH is among the variables it carries.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033{\cf1
We use extracytoplasmic to describe the outward-facing side of the cytoplasmic membrane across the bacterial sample. In diderm bacteria these positions face the periplasm; in monoderms they face the cell wall and extracellular environment. Position classes are transferred from reference annotations by sequence alignment, rather than measured structurally in each sampled organism.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240{\cf1
We test which genomic features are associated with reported minimum growth pH after adjustment for growth optimum, and whether the association differs between outward-facing and cytoplasmic positions of the same proteins.}
\par \pard\plain \s1\sl480\slmult1\keep\sb240\sa80\keepn\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs28\ab\ltrch\hich\af5\afs28\ab\dbch\af10\loch\f5\fs28\b{\cf1\f8
Materials & Methods}
\par \pard\plain \s2\sl480\slmult1\keep\sb240\sa80\keepn\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs26\ab\ltrch\hich\af5\afs24\ab\dbch\af10\loch\f5\fs24\b{\cf1\hich\af8\afs24\dbch\af11\loch\f8\fs24
Phenotype data}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240{\cf1\hich\ai0\dbch\af11\loch\i0
The analysis sample is 2,768 bacterial genomes carrying a curated minimum growth pH and a position in the reference phylogeny. Minima were compiled from four source tiers, recorded per record: hand-curated species descriptions read from primary sources, rule-based extraction from species-description abstracts, ThermoBase, and AciDB/BacDive [9]. Source is entered as a set of indicator covariates in every model, so a between-source level difference cannot by itself produce an association.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240{\cf1\hich\ai0\dbch\af11\loch\i0
The extraction tier dominates the sample and its fidelity was measured rather than assumed. Against hand-curated records it is exact for 98.8% of minima. Because that validation set was acidophile-enriched, 25 further records were read by hand from the pH band carrying most of the data; 3 of 25 were wrong, all by one mechanism, in which a sentence reporting only an optimum has that optimum recorded as the growth range. A detector for that specific failure scores 8 of 8 against fifteen hand-read statements with no false positives and flags 298 of 2,536 extracted records; excluding exactly those records moves the headline estimate by 0.13 standard errors.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033{\cf1
One assembly was retained per species. The bacterial phenotype table contains 2,771 assemblies; intersection with the reference phylogeny produces the 2,768-genome headline sample. Before that intersection, source tiers contain 2,536 automatically extracted records, 100 ThermoBase records, 90 hand-curated records, 30 AciDB records and 15 BacDive records. Sources can overlap; the deposited table records the selected source and assembly provenance. The hand-curated tier is enriched for acidophiles and is not a representative random validation set.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033{\cf1
The 25-record central-range check found three optimum-as-range extraction errors. The targeted detector was assessed on 15 hand-read statements and flagged 298 extracted records. The primary analysis retains the deposited phenotype table; exclusion of flagged records is a sensitivity analysis, not a correction of those records. Only five species were recorded as having a strictly demonstrated lower limit in the project curation. Cross-source agreement largely measures agreement in transcription of shared literature and does not establish the precision of the original growth experiments.}
\par \pard\plain \s2\sl480\slmult1\keep\sb240\sa80\keepn\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs26\ab\ltrch\hich\af5\afs24\ab\dbch\af10\loch\f5\fs24\b{\cf1\hich\af8\afs24\dbch\af11\loch\f8\fs24
Structural panel and composite}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240{\cf1
The predictor is built on a panel of conserved inner-membrane proteins of }{\cf1\i
Escherichia coli}{\cf1
 with experimentally determined or high-confidence predicted structures. Each reference position was assigned to a class \u8212\'97 exposed extracytoplasmic, exposed cytoplasmic, transmembrane or buried \u8212\'97 from UniProt topology together with relative solvent accessibility of the isolated model, computed by the Shrake\u8211\'96Rupley algorithm [18] and normalised to the maxima of Tien et al. [10], with exposure defined as RSA \u8805\'3f 0.25. A position enters only if its per-residue pLDDT is \u8805\'3f 70, since the solvent accessibility of a poorly predicted residue is not interpretable. For each genome, the best-aligning hit of the reference's Pfam family was tallied by the class of the reference position it aligned to, counting D, E, K, R and H separately.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240{\cf1
The composite is the mean across references of the per-reference net acidic share (D + E \u8722\'3f K \u8722\'3f R) divided by scorable positions, each reference standardised across genomes before averaging, with a per-reference position floor and a minimum of three contributing references. The panel used throughout is a 32-reference rule-selected set; a 7-reference hand-picked panel preceded it and is not the same measurement, so per-unit coefficients from the two are never compared. Canonical and deterministic panel provenance is distinguished below; the canonical stored panel is not a byte-identical rebuild of the corrected code.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033{\cf1
Reference selection used reviewed }{\cf1\i
E. coli}{\cf1
 K-12 proteins with at least four transmembrane helices, at least three annotated outward-facing and three cytoplasmic topological domains, and exactly one Pfam family. One candidate per family was retained using the number of annotated topological domains. Screening used 150 sampled bacterial proteomes, an 80% detection threshold, median model pLDDT of at least 70, and at least 15 exposed outward-facing positions after residue-confidence filtering. Accepted reference identifiers and annotations are deposited in data/curated/structure_panel_refs.csv; five conserved soluble references serve as controls.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033{\cf1
For the headline composite, a reference contributes only when the number of scorable positions reaches max(8, half the reference class length). Its net acidic share is (D + E \u8722\'3f K \u8722\'3f R)/n. Each reference share is centred and scaled across the available genomes, then available standardised shares are averaged; at least three references must contribute. Model rows are restricted to complete outcomes, predictors and covariates; missing growth optima are not imputed. The genome-wide composition control is acidic excess, (D + E \u8722\'3f K \u8722\'3f R), expressed as percentage points. Source indicators use hand-curated literature as the reference category.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033{\cf1
A separate analysis pools counts across reference proteins, requires at least 200 outward-facing exposed positions, and models net residue share together with exposed histidine. It is a different predictor from the mean of standardised reference shares. Retained percentages are calculated within that pooled-share analysis and must not be computed by dividing its coefficient by a coefficient from the headline composite. The exact model samples, scales, coefficients and standard errors are recorded in headline_numbers.csv and optimum_and_histidine_wide.csv.}
\par \pard\plain \s2\sl480\slmult1\keep\sb240\sa80\keepn\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs26\ab\ltrch\hich\af5\afs24\ab\dbch\af10\loch\f5\fs24\b{\cf1\hich\af8\afs24\dbch\af11\loch\f8\fs24
Statistical model}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240{\cf1\hich\ai0\dbch\af11\loch\i0
All estimates are phylogenetic generalised least squares on a Brownian covariance matrix derived from the reference marker-gene tree, with Pagel's \u955\'3f [11] estimated by maximum likelihood. Every model carries an intercept, a genome-wide amino acid composition control and the source indicators. Coefficients are reported per standard deviation of the predictor unless a per-unit figure is explicitly labelled, because composites averaging different numbers of references have different spreads. Where a model adds the growth optimum, the comparison is made on the single sample for which the optimum is known, never against the full-sample figure, so that a change of model is not confounded with a change of sample. Multiple testing across reference families is controlled by the Benjamini\u8211\'96Hochberg procedure [12].}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240{\cf1\hich\ai0\dbch\af11\loch\i0
Compartment contrasts are estimated by entering both faces in one model as their sum and difference. This spans the same column space as entering the two levels and so changes no fitted value, but it makes the extracytoplasmic excess an estimate carrying its own standard error, which a difference taken between two separately fitted coefficients does not.}
\par \pard\plain \s2\sl480\slmult1\keep\sb240\sa80\keepn\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs26\ab\ltrch\hich\af5\afs24\ab\dbch\af10\loch\f5\fs24\b{\cf1\f8
Phylogeny construction and sensitivity}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033{\cf1
The phylogeny uses 29 ribosomal-protein Pfam marker families. Highest-scoring gathering-cutoff hits were aligned to their Pfam profiles with pyHMMER hmmalign using match columns and concatenated. Genomes with fewer than half the retained families and columns with less than 50% occupancy were removed. Gamma-corrected Poisson distances on shared non-gap sites were used for neighbour joining, followed by midpoint rooting. The marker list, assembly order and retained tree are deposited with the code. The Brownian covariance is shared root-to-tip path length; Pagel\u8217\'92s \u955\'3f scales its off-diagonal entries and is estimated by maximum likelihood.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033{\cf1
The headline association remained significant on 25 column-bootstrap trees, with coefficients ranging from +0.1751 to +0.1863 per standard deviation (median +0.1822). These trees share an alignment and inference method. No maximum-likelihood or approximate-maximum-likelihood tree sensitivity analysis was completed for the full 2,768-genome sample; shared systematic topology or branch-length error remains a limitation. Parametric null calibration and bootstrap analyses assess specified assumptions, rather than proving the phylogenetic model correct.}
\par \pard\plain \s2\sl480\slmult1\keep\sb240\sa80\keepn\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs26\ab\ltrch\hich\af5\afs24\ab\dbch\af10\loch\f5\fs24\b{\cf1\f8
Panel provenance and analysis scope}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033{\cf1
The canonical stored panel predates deterministic paralogue tie-breaking. A corrected build sorts tied hits and output rows and reproduces byte-identically on repeated runs, but the canonical results have not all been migrated to that build. Refitting the authoritative headline table on the corrected panel changes +0.1848 to +0.1858 per standard deviation (0.035 standard errors), with no sign changes or p = 0.05 crossings among its 14 rows. This comparison bounds that measured impact; it does not make the original stored panel a byte-identical rebuild. Independent estimator replication begins from stored intermediates and does not independently validate phenotype curation, hit detection or structural annotation.}
\par \pard\plain \s2\sl480\slmult1\keep\sb240\sa80\keepn\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs26\ab\ltrch\hich\af5\afs24\ab\dbch\af10\loch\f5\fs24\b{\cf1\hich\af8\afs24\dbch\af11\loch\f8\fs24
Verification}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240{\cf1
Verification used a static audit for nine previously encountered code-error classes, 26 unit tests, numerical traceability checks on the working Markdown manuscript and supporting documents, and an independently implemented PGLS estimator. The traceability check verifies that quoted numbers occur in a cited result table, but does not establish that a number supports its attached interpretation; it does not directly parse this Word document. The independent estimator shares no Newick parser, covariance construction, matrix factorisation or \u955\'3f search with the original. Across 30 declared comparisons, its headline estimate is +0.184838 per standard deviation compared with +0.184837 in the original. These checks assess specified computational properties rather than independently validating all biological inputs.}
\par \pard\plain \s1\sl480\slmult1\keep\sb240\sa80\keepn\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs28\ab\ltrch\hich\af5\afs28\ab\dbch\af10\loch\f5\fs28\b{\cf1\hich\af8\afs28\dbch\af11\loch\f8\fs28
Results}
\par \pard\plain \s2\sl480\slmult1\keep\sb240\sa80\keepn\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs26\ab\ltrch\hich\af5\afs24\ab\dbch\af10\loch\f5\fs24\b{\cf1\f8
Exposed outward-facing composition is associated with reported minimum growth pH}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240{\cf1\hich\ai0\dbch\af11\loch\i0
The composite of net acidic residue share at exposed extracytoplasmic positions gives +0.1848 pH units per standard deviation (2,768 genomes, P = 2 \u215\'d7 10\u8315\'3f\u185\'b9\u8304\'3f) with genome-wide composition and source controlled (Fig. 1). The sign warrants stating explicitly, because it is easy to invert in prose: the coefficient is positive, so a higher net acidic share accompanies a higher minimum, that is, less acid tolerance. Acid-tolerant bacteria are depleted of acidic residues on their outward-facing extracytoplasmic surfaces, not enriched. At compartment level the contrast is direct: genomes with pH_min \u8804\'3f 3.5 average a secreted acidic share of 0.0999 against 0.1246 for those at or above pH 6.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\qc\sb120\sa80\keepn

\par \pard\plain \s2\sl480\slmult1\keep\sb240\sa80\keepn\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs26\ab\ltrch\hich\af5\afs24\ab\dbch\af10\loch\f5\fs24\b{\cf1\f8
Associations differ between membrane position classes}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240{\cf1\hich\ai0\dbch\af11\loch\i0
Because the claim is about a compartment, the controls are the same measurement computed on other compartments of the same proteins, on the same genomes, in the same model (Fig. 1). Exposed cytoplasmic gives +0.0212 (P = 0.33) and transmembrane -0.0430 (P = 0.07); both are null. The soluble-surface reference set gives -0.0935 (P = 0.01), which is significant and opposite in sign. That is not a failure of specificity, but it does mean the pattern is a contrast between compartments rather than a periplasm-only effect, and we describe it as such.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240{\cf1\hich\ai0\dbch\af11\loch\i0
Entering both faces in a single model as sum and difference makes the extracytoplasmic excess an estimate in its own right: +0.0536 pH per percentage point (P = 3 \u215\'d7 10\u8315\'3f\u8310\'3f).}
\par \pard\plain \s2\sl480\slmult1\keep\sb240\sa80\keepn\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs26\ab\ltrch\hich\af5\afs24\ab\dbch\af10\loch\f5\fs24\b{\cf1\hich\af8\afs24\dbch\af11\loch\f8\fs24
Part of the association is independent of growth optimum and of histidine}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240{\cf1
On the same 1,655 genomes with a recorded optimum, the headline mean-of-standardised-shares composite gives +0.2123 per standard deviation with composition and source controlled and +0.0904 with growth optimum also controlled. Figure 2 presents a separate pooled-share analysis on these genomes: adding growth optimum and exposed histidine together leaves +0.1120 per standard deviation (P = 4 \u215\'d7 10\u8315\'3f\u179\'b3), or 39% of that pooled predictor\u8217\'92s own baseline coefficient. These coefficients come from different predictor definitions and should not be divided to estimate a retained percentage. The product of the pooled analysis\u8217\'92s separate retained shares is 0.375, compared with a joint share of 0.387.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\qc\sb120\sa80\keepn

\par \pard\plain \s2\sl480\slmult1\keep\sb240\sa80\keepn\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs26\ab\ltrch\hich\af5\afs24\ab\dbch\af10\loch\f5\fs24\b{\cf1\hich\af8\afs24\dbch\af11\loch\f8\fs24
Exposed histidine is a separate association, not a proxy}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240{\cf1\hich\ai0\dbch\af11\loch\i0
Exposed extracytoplasmic histidine share carries a larger coefficient than the acidic composite and in the opposite direction. Entered as sum and difference across the two compartments, with extracytoplasmic D, E and K held fixed, the shared component is -0.1317 pH per percentage point and the extracytoplasmic excess -0.0800 (P = 3 \u215\'d7 10\u8315\'3f\u179\'b3) \u8212\'97 so the association has both a side-specific and a shared part. That contrast was calibrated on the acidic result first, which fires at +0.0536, because a test is only a test once it has been shown capable of firing.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240{\cf1\hich\ai0\dbch\af11\loch\i0
The two measures correlate at r = \u8722\'3f0.549, which makes one standing in for the other the obvious concern. Mutual adjustment excludes it: each retains roughly three quarters of its coefficient with the other held fixed, and the acidic measure's one-sidedness survives at 77% of its unadjusted value. Solvent access is doing demonstrable work \u8212\'97 histidine at non-exposed positions in the same reference families predicts nothing, alone or jointly. A previously specified ordering of coefficients by side-chain pKa does not hold monotonically; what does organise them is charge class, which remains significant with histidine held fixed, while pKa orders the acidic pair and fails to order the basic residues.}
\par \pard\plain \s2\sl480\slmult1\keep\sb240\sa80\keepn\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs26\ab\ltrch\hich\af5\afs24\ab\dbch\af10\loch\f5\fs24\b{\cf1\f8
Detectable reference families are associated with reported minimum growth pH}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240{\cf1\hich\ai0\dbch\af11\loch\i0
How many of the panel's reference families a genome has a detectable homologue of was initially held fixed as a nuisance control. Decomposed, it is two things: breadth survives at \u8722\'3f0.036 pH per reference with assembly level, similarity to the reference proteins and genome size held fixed, whereas depth \u8212\'97 positions per homologue \u8212\'97 carries nothing once similarity is controlled. Of the accepted references, 22 have enough detection variance to regress on and 7 survive Benjamini\u8211\'96Hochberg at q < 0.10 (Fig. 3), the strongest being cytochrome bo(3) ubiquinol oxidase subunit 1 at -0.4286 pH.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\qc\sb120\sa80\keepn

\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240{\cf1
Three limits are explicit. The intended negative control cannot be run: the soluble reference proteins are detected in over 99% of genomes and therefore fall below the variance floor, so none was tested, and a control that cannot run is a limitation rather than a pass. What replaces it is weaker and labelled so \u8212\'97 the survivors' signs are mixed, five down and two up, which argues against a uniform directional artefact but does not exclude family-specific detection bias or establish function. Detection is a crude proxy for presence, so these are claims about detectable homologues of }{\cf1\i
E. coli}{\cf1
 proteins. And breadth retains 47% of its coefficient with the survivors held fixed against a pre-set criterion of half, so the named references carry about half of the breadth effect rather than being it.}
\par \pard\plain \s2\sl480\slmult1\keep\sb240\sa80\keepn\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs26\ab\ltrch\hich\af5\afs24\ab\dbch\af10\loch\f5\fs24\b{\cf1\hich\af8\afs24\dbch\af11\loch\f8\fs24
The measure survives being made homogeneous, and the estimator reproduces}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240{\cf1
The contribution of reference families varies between genomes, so holding reference breadth fixed does not guarantee that every genome\u8217\'92s composite measures identical positions. Sensitivity analyses use a six-reference common core and the 239 genomes carrying the complete reference set; the corresponding associations are +0.0362 and +0.0888 pH units per percentage point. These results support stability under those restrictions, without establishing that reference-level measurements are interchangeable.}
\par \pard\plain \s1\sl480\slmult1\keep\sb240\sa80\keepn\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs28\ab\ltrch\hich\af5\afs28\ab\dbch\af10\loch\f5\fs28\b{\cf1\hich\af8\afs28\dbch\af11\loch\f8\fs28
Discussion}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240{\cf1\hich\ai0\dbch\af11\loch\i0
Three associations survive the controls designed to break them, and the compartment specificity is the part we would defend most firmly: the cytoplasmic and transmembrane controls are the identical measurement on other faces of the same proteins, and they are null. We think the limits below matter as much as the findings.}
\par \pard\plain \s2\sl480\slmult1\keep\sb240\sa80\keepn\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs26\ab\ltrch\hich\af5\afs24\ab\dbch\af10\loch\f5\fs24\b{\cf1\f8
Charge burden and buffering hypotheses}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240{\cf1\hich\ai0\dbch\af11\loch\i0
The mechanism is contested by the one directly comparable experiment. Weeks and Sachs mutagenised every histidine, aspartate and glutamate in the extracytoplasmic domain of the }{\cf1\hich\ai\dbch\af11\loch\i
H. pylori}{\cf1\hich\ai0\dbch\af11\loch\i0
 urea channel UreI and found three histidines and three carboxylates all required to activate transport at acidic pH, with those residues largely absent from the pH-independent }{\cf1\hich\ai\dbch\af11\loch\i
Streptococcus salivarius}{\cf1\hich\ai0\dbch\af11\loch\i0
 homologue [3]. There, extracytoplasmic carboxylates are required for low-pH function; here they are depleted in acid-tolerant genomes. A later study adds a conserved cytoplasmic glutamate to the same channel's gating [13], and an engineering study aimed at gastric pH deliberately adds acidic residues on an explicit buffering rationale [14].}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240{\cf1
We contrasted simplified charge-burden and buffering hypotheses using composition-derived titration summaries; these summaries do not identify a mechanism on their own. Writing Q(pH) for net surface charge and \u946\'3f(pH) = \u8722\'3fdQ/dpH for buffer capacity, a charge-burden account predicts acid tolerance accompanying a smaller charge change between neutrality and the limit, while a buffering account predicts it accompanying greater capacity at the operating pH. At pH 3 and pH 4, inside the acidophile range and near the carboxylate pKa values, the charge change carries a positive coefficient (+0.1399, P = 4 \u215\'d7 10\u8315\'3f\u8312\'3f; +0.0776, P = 1 \u215\'d7 10\u8315\'3f\u179\'b3), so acid-tolerant genomes have less outward-facing charge to change, as burden predicts, while buffer capacity carries a positive coefficient, the opposite of what buffering predicts. Both quantities reverse sign between pH 4 and pH 6 (-0.2092), which is where histidine titrates and where its opposite-signed association appears inside the same quantity. We emphasise that this is not an electrostatics calculation \u8212\'97 no structure, no solvent, no interaction between groups, and model pKa values throughout.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240{\cf1\hich\ai0\dbch\af11\loch\i0
A protein whose function is to respond to acid needs titratable groups on the face that senses it; a proteome that must merely continue working at low pH may do better with fewer. That reconciles UreI with the present result, and it makes a falsifiable prediction: a panel built from dedicated pH sensors should carry the opposite sign to a housekeeping panel. We attempted that test and it cannot be run on this sample. Dedicated extracytoplasmic sensor domains are detected in 1.9% of genomes, with all detections in a single phylum, so the contrast would be between lineages rather than between protein classes. The prediction is untested, not refuted.}
\par \pard\plain \s2\sl480\slmult1\keep\sb240\sa80\keepn\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs26\ab\ltrch\hich\af5\afs24\ab\dbch\af10\loch\f5\fs24\b{\cf1\f8
Reported minima and censoring sensitivity}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240{\cf1\hich\ai0\dbch\af11\loch\i0
pH_min is meant to be the lowest pH at which an organism grows. What a species description usually records is the lowest pH at which growth was observed, bounded below by whatever the authors chose to test. If that choice tracks what they expected, the error is not noise but is systematically related to apparent acid tolerance and therefore to anything predicting it.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240{\cf1\hich\ai0\dbch\af11\loch\i0
The reported minima do carry a testing-grid signature: 74% sit on a whole pH unit and 93% on a whole or half unit, and the modal value, pH 6.0, accounts for 34% of the sample. A measured limit has no reason to prefer a round number; a testing grid has every reason to. Measured outcome reliability is high (ICC 0.965 from cross-source replicate readings, error SD 0.343 pH) and supports the standard result that error in the dependent variable inflates standard errors without biasing slopes \u8212\'97 but that holds for classical error, independent of the predictor by construction. Censoring is not classical: it has a sign and a mechanism. Simulated at equal magnitude, classical noise moves the coefficient by 0.006 standard errors while the same magnitude aligned with the predictor moves it by 11.8.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\qc\sb120\sa80\keepn

\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\qc\sb120\sa80\keepn

\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240{\cf1
We evaluated specified observable censoring proxies. Erasing the headline slope requires censoring displacement to change by 0.185 pH units per predictor standard deviation (Fig. 4A). For the two proxies with effects in the threatening direction, the required mean displacements are at least 8.4 pH units, exceeding the roughly 6.0-unit headroom under an assumed lower bound of pH 0 (Fig. 4B). This bound applies only to those proxies and that assumed floor; pH 0 is not a universal physical lower limit. The proxies are weak and may capture other traits. Predictor-related censoring not represented by these proxies, or selection of organisms for description, remains unexcluded. Common-grid measurements or full-text classification of bound type would provide a stronger test.}
\par \pard\plain \s2\sl480\slmult1\keep\sb240\sa80\keepn\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs26\ab\ltrch\hich\af5\afs24\ab\dbch\af10\loch\f5\fs24\b{\cf1\f8
Reference transfer and envelope architecture}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033{\cf1
The reference models are isolated chains and do not represent every oligomeric interface or species-specific structure. For alternative reference structures from the same Pfam family, per-genome measurements correlate weakly (median r = 0.126), although rescoring the same reference is reproducible. Topology-only annotation retains approximately 101% of the headline effect; solvent exposure concentrates signal per position but is not required for the aggregate association. The strongest interpretation is an outward-facing versus cytoplasmic contrast, rather than an exposure-specific causal effect.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033{\cf1
The association is present in both diderm and monoderm subsets. In a ten-phylum meta-regression, the monoderm increment is +0.1239 per standard deviation (SE 0.0788; one-sided P = 0.058). Envelope classification is coarse and partly confounded with phylogeny. These comparisons motivate consistent use of extracytoplasmic terminology and do not establish that outer-membrane architecture modifies the effect.}
\par \pard\plain \s2\sl480\slmult1\keep\sb240\sa80\keepn\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs26\ab\ltrch\hich\af5\afs24\ab\dbch\af10\loch\f5\fs24\b{\cf1\hich\af8\afs24\dbch\af11\loch\f8\fs24
What the association does not do}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240{\cf1
It does not predict out of clade. Fitting on every other phylum, with the composite re-standardised on training genomes only, pooled out-of-sample RMSE is 0.9253 pH with the composite against 0.9248 pH without it \u8212\'97 fractionally worse \u8212\'97 and the cytoplasmic control, at 0.9238 pH, does no worse than either. The composite lowers error in three of four folds, and the pooled figure is decided by the one it fails, which is the phylum holding most of the acidophiles and whose removal leaves the training set with little of the acid-tolerant range the effect is confined to; fold-level gain tracks the tail retained in training at r = +0.835 across four folds, a direction predicted in advance and resting on four points. Both readings stand: the association survives every within-sample test reported here and buys no out-of-sample accuracy across clades. These results support an in-sample conditional association and do not demonstrate generalisable prediction or a causal mechanism. Reference selection was additionally performed once on all genomes, so even these figures are optimistic by an unmeasured amount.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240{\cf1\hich\ai0\dbch\af11\loch\i0
The effects are small in variance terms, well under 1% of pH_min variance beyond the growth optimum, and the relationship is concentrated in the acid-tolerant tail rather than running uniformly across the range. Nothing here is interventional. In archaea the pattern is consistent but marginal, and within halophilic archaea specificity fails outright.}
\par \pard\plain \s2\sl480\slmult1\keep\sb240\sa80\keepn\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs26\ab\ltrch\hich\af5\afs24\ab\dbch\af10\loch\f5\fs24\b{\cf1\hich\af8\afs24\dbch\af11\loch\f8\fs24
A second environmental variable acts on a different compartment}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240{\cf1\hich\ai0\dbch\af11\loch\i0
Because compartment-resolved isoelectric point shifts with marine-versus-freshwater habitat in the same ordering reported here [7], a habitat variable could in principle act through the extracytoplasmic face and mimic the result. Measured salinity tolerance, available for 669 genomes of this sample, does not predict pH_min (P = 0.36) and removes 1% of the extracytoplasmic coefficient when held fixed. More informative, the two variables have separable compartment profiles: salinity gives secreted > cytoplasmic > membrane, reproducing the published ordering, while pH_min gives secreted > membrane > cytoplasmic. Salinity moves the secreted and cytoplasmic shares together, as a whole-cell osmotic account predicts; pH moves the secreted share and leaves the cytoplasmic one untouched. The method does not impose one ordering on every variable given to it, which is a stronger defence of the controls than salinity being inert \u8212\'97 it is not inert, and predicts secreted composition more strongly than pH does. Within the salinity-measured subsample the transmembrane control does fire, and that signal is confined to the salt-tolerant half, consistent with halotolerant membranes being compositionally distinct: a second environmental variable perturbing a different compartment from the one claimed here.}
\par \pard\plain \s1\sl480\slmult1\keep\sb240\sa80\keepn\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs28\ab\ltrch\hich\af5\afs28\ab\dbch\af10\loch\f5\fs28\b{\cf1\f8
Conclusions}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240{\cf1
Extracytoplasmic-facing charge composition in the reference panel is associated with reported minimum growth pH after adjustment for growth optimum and histidine. The direction is lower net acidic composition in acid-tolerant organisms. Composition-derived titration summaries are consistent with a charge-burden hypothesis, but do not establish it over alternative mechanisms. The outcome is shaped by growth-testing conventions, the phylogeny and structural annotation impose additional assumptions, and the composite provides no out-of-clade predictive gain. An experimental charge-reversal protocol has been specified but not performed; causal interpretation awaits experimental evidence.}
\par \pard\plain \s1\sl480\slmult1\keep\sb240\sa80\keepn\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs28\ab\ltrch\hich\af5\afs28\ab\dbch\af10\loch\f5\fs28\b{\cf1\hich\af8\dbch\af11\loch\f8
Data availability}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240{\cf1
The code, curated inputs, stored results and figures are available at https://github.com/ajayvellanki0720-boop/acidophile-ph-limits-submission. The code and reproducibility archive (version 1.0.0, commit 97257f22dde358b920c70a67aa921a08556b41fb) is preserved at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24147308. Supplemental Data S1 is the accompanying analysis-data archive, containing curated phenotype and accession tables, reference annotations, stored model inputs and results, phylogenies, environment specifications, provenance and SHA-256 checksums. It is supplied for direct upload as journal-hosted supplemental material. Oversized third-party raw genomes, proteomes and structures are accessed through the accession and retrieval instructions in DATA_AVAILABILITY.md; no new sequence data were generated. The repository documents which reconstruction steps require those inputs and manual curation.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240{\cf1
No new sequence data were generated by this study. Genome assemblies and proteomes were obtained from NCBI [16]; the accession used for every genome is listed in the deposited file accession_used.csv, which should be cited alongside the originating submitters of those assemblies.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240{\cf1
Growth phenotypes were compiled from published species descriptions and from the following databases, whose authors should be credited by any reuse: AciDB (acidb.cl), ThermoBase, BacDive, and metaTraits (metatraits.embl.de) [15], the last supplying the measured salinity values used in the confounder analysis. Protein structures were obtained from the AlphaFold Protein Structure Database [17] and Pfam/InterPro hidden Markov models from EMBL-EBI.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\sl360\slmult1\widctlpar\sb0\sa240\ltrpar\hyphpar0\kerning0\cf1\rtlch\af13\afs22\alang1025\ltrch\hich\af8\afs24\alang1033\dbch\af11\langfe1033\loch\f8\fs24\lang1033\sl360\slmult1\sb0\sa240{\cf1
